@{
    Layout = "~/Areas/HTML/Views/Shared/HTMLLayout.cshtml";
    ViewData["Title"] = "HTML5 Audio and Video";
}

<style>
    .content-section {
        line-height: 1.8;
    }

    .content-section h2 {
        color: #212529 !important;
        font-weight: 700;
        margin-top: 24px;
        margin-bottom: 14px;
    }

    .content-section h3 {
        color: #374151;
        font-weight: 600;
        margin-top: 20px;
        margin-bottom: 12px;
    }

    .content-section p {
        color: #4b5563;
    }

    pre {
        background-color: #212529 !important;
        color: #fff !important;
        border-radius: 8px;
        padding: 16px !important;
        overflow-x: auto;
        margin-top: 12px;
    }

    pre code {
        background: transparent !important;
        color: #fff !important;
    }

    .info-box {
        background: #f3f4ff;
        border-left: 4px solid #4f46e5;
        padding: 16px;
        border-radius: 6px;
        margin: 18px 0;
    }

    .diagram-box {
        border: 1px solid #dee2e6;
        border-radius: 10px;
        padding: 24px;
        background: #ffffff;
        margin: 20px 0;
    }

    .diagram-title {
        text-align: center;
        font-weight: 700;
        color: #212529;
        margin-bottom: 18px;
    }

    .media-flow {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: center;
        gap: 12px;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
    }

    .flow-item {
        border: 1px solid #dee2e6;
        background: #f8f9fa;
        padding: 14px 18px;
        border-radius: 8px;
        text-align: center;
        min-width: 120px;
        font-weight: 600;
    }

    .flow-arrow {
        font-size: 22px;
        font-weight: 700;
        color: #212529;
    }

    .demo-box {
        border: 1px solid #dee2e6;
        background: #ffffff;
        padding: 20px;
        border-radius: 8px;
        margin: 18px 0;
    }

    .table th {
        background-color: #f8f9fa;
    }
</style>

<main id="main-content" class="container py-5">
    <div class="row g-4">

        <!-- Left Sidebar -->
        <div class="col-lg-3">
            @Html.Partial("~/Areas/HTML/Views/Shared/HTMLTopics.cshtml")
        </div>

        <!-- Main Content -->
        <div class="col-lg-9 content-section">

            <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">HTML5 Audio and Video</h1>

            <p>
                HTML5 provides built-in elements for adding audio and video directly
                to a webpage. The two main elements are
                <code>&lt;audio&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;video&gt;</code>.
            </p>

            <div class="info-box">
                <strong>Simple Idea:</strong>
                <code>&lt;audio&gt;</code> is used for sound,
                while <code>&lt;video&gt;</code> is used for moving visual content.
            </div>

            <h2>1. Audio and Video at a Glance</h2>

            <div class="diagram-box">
                <div class="diagram-title">
                    HTML5 Multimedia
                </div>

                <div class="media-flow">

                    <div class="flow-item">
                        HTML5
                    </div>

                    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>

                    <div class="flow-item">
                        &lt;audio&gt;<br>
                        Sound
                    </div>

                    <div class="flow-arrow">+</div>

                    <div class="flow-item">
                        &lt;video&gt;<br>
                        Video
                    </div>

                    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>

                    <div class="flow-item">
                        Browser<br>
                        Playback
                    </div>

                </div>
            </div>

            <h2>2. The audio Element</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>&lt;audio&gt;</code> element is used to embed sound content
                such as music, voice recordings, podcasts, and other audio files.
            </p>

            <p>
                A simple example is:
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;audio controls&gt;
    &lt;source src="music.mp3" type="audio/mpeg"&gt;
&lt;/audio&gt;</code></pre>

            <p>
                The <code>controls</code> attribute tells the browser to display
                playback controls.
            </p>

            <h2>3. Audio Controls</h2>

            <p>
                Without the <code>controls</code> attribute, the browser does not
                normally display its standard playback controls.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;audio controls&gt;
    &lt;source src="lesson.mp3" type="audio/mpeg"&gt;
&lt;/audio&gt;</code></pre>

            <p>
                Depending on the browser, the controls can provide options such as
                play, pause, volume, and playback position.
            </p>

            <h2>4. Multiple Audio Sources</h2>

            <p>
                A source can be provided using the <code>&lt;source&gt;</code> element.
                Multiple sources can be listed so the browser can use a compatible one.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;audio controls&gt;

    &lt;source src="lesson.mp3"
            type="audio/mpeg"&gt;

    &lt;source src="lesson.ogg"
            type="audio/ogg"&gt;

&lt;/audio&gt;</code></pre>

            <div class="info-box">
                <strong>Why multiple sources?</strong>
                Different browsers or environments may support different media formats.
                The browser can choose a supported source.
            </div>

            <h2>5. Audio with Autoplay</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>autoplay</code> attribute requests automatic playback.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;audio controls autoplay&gt;

    &lt;source src="music.mp3"
            type="audio/mpeg"&gt;

&lt;/audio&gt;</code></pre>

            <p>
                Modern browsers may restrict automatic playback, especially when
                sound would begin without user interaction.
            </p>

            <h2>6. Audio with Loop</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>loop</code> attribute causes the media to start again
                when it reaches the end.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;audio controls loop&gt;

    &lt;source src="background.mp3"
            type="audio/mpeg"&gt;

&lt;/audio&gt;</code></pre>

            <h2>7. Muted Audio</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>muted</code> attribute starts the audio in a muted state.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;audio controls muted&gt;

    &lt;source src="audio.mp3"
            type="audio/mpeg"&gt;

&lt;/audio&gt;</code></pre>

            <h2>8. The video Element</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>&lt;video&gt;</code> element is used to embed video content
                directly into a webpage.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;video controls width="500"&gt;

    &lt;source src="lesson.mp4"
            type="video/mp4"&gt;

&lt;/video&gt;</code></pre>

            <p>
                The browser can provide controls for playing and managing the video.
            </p>

            <h2>9. Video with Width and Height</h2>

            <p>
                The size of a video can be controlled using attributes such as
                <code>width</code> and <code>height</code>.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;video controls
       width="600"
       height="350"&gt;

    &lt;source src="lesson.mp4"
            type="video/mp4"&gt;

&lt;/video&gt;</code></pre>

            <h2>10. Video Poster</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>poster</code> attribute specifies an image that can be
                displayed before the video starts playing.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;video controls
       poster="thumbnail.jpg"
       width="600"&gt;

    &lt;source src="lesson.mp4"
            type="video/mp4"&gt;

&lt;/video&gt;</code></pre>

            <p>
                A poster image is useful because it gives users a visual preview
                before playback starts.
            </p>

            <h2>11. Video with Multiple Sources</h2>

            <pre><code>&lt;video controls width="600"&gt;

    &lt;source src="lesson.mp4"
            type="video/mp4"&gt;

    &lt;source src="lesson.webm"
            type="video/webm"&gt;

&lt;/video&gt;</code></pre>

            <h2>12. Video with Autoplay</h2>

            <p>
                The <code>autoplay</code> attribute requests automatic playback.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;video controls autoplay&gt;

    &lt;source src="lesson.mp4"
            type="video/mp4"&gt;

&lt;/video&gt;</code></pre>

            <p>
                Browser policies can affect whether autoplay is actually allowed.
            </p>

            <h2>13. Video with Loop and Muted</h2>

            <p>
                These attributes are often combined when a video should repeat
                continuously and start without sound.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;video controls
       autoplay
       loop
       muted&gt;

    &lt;source src="intro.mp4"
            type="video/mp4"&gt;

&lt;/video&gt;</code></pre>

            <h2>14. Audio vs Video</h2>

            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table class="table table-bordered align-middle">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Feature</th>
                            <th>Audio</th>
                            <th>Video</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>

                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>Element</td>
                            <td><code>&lt;audio&gt;</code></td>
                            <td><code>&lt;video&gt;</code></td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Purpose</td>
                            <td>Sound content</td>
                            <td>Video content</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Visual Content</td>
                            <td>No</td>
                            <td>Yes</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Controls</td>
                            <td>Supported</td>
                            <td>Supported</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>Source</td>
                            <td>Audio file</td>
                            <td>Video file</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <h2>15. Fallback Text</h2>

            <p>
                Text can be placed inside an audio or video element as fallback
                content for browsers that cannot use the media element.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;video controls&gt;

    &lt;source src="lesson.mp4"
            type="video/mp4"&gt;

    Your browser does not support video playback.

&lt;/video&gt;</code></pre>

            <h2>16. Audio and Video Attributes</h2>

            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table class="table table-bordered align-middle">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Attribute</th>
                            <th>Purpose</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>

                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>controls</code></td>
                            <td>Displays playback controls.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>autoplay</code></td>
                            <td>Requests automatic playback.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>loop</code></td>
                            <td>Repeats the media.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>muted</code></td>
                            <td>Starts the media muted.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>poster</code></td>
                            <td>Displays a preview image for video.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>width</code></td>
                            <td>Controls video width.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>height</code></td>
                            <td>Controls video height.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <h2>17. Example: Training Video</h2>

            <p>
                A training website can use the video element to show recorded lessons.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;video controls
       poster="html-course.jpg"
       width="700"&gt;

    &lt;source src="html-lesson.mp4"
            type="video/mp4"&gt;

    Your browser does not support video playback.

&lt;/video&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <h2>18. Example: Training Audio</h2>

            <p>
                Audio can be useful for voice lessons, podcasts, or recorded explanations.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;audio controls&gt;

    &lt;source src="html-explanation.mp3"
            type="audio/mpeg"&gt;

    Your browser does not support audio playback.

&lt;/audio&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>


            <h2>19. Common Mistakes</h2>

            <h3>Mistake 1: Forgetting controls</h3>

            <p>
                Without <code>controls</code>, users may not see the standard
                playback interface.
            </p>

            <h3>Mistake 2: Providing the wrong file type</h3>

            <p>
                The file format and declared MIME type should match the media file.
            </p>

            <h3>Mistake 3: Depending on autoplay</h3>

            <p>
                Autoplay behavior can be restricted by browsers, especially for
                media that starts with sound.
            </p>

            <h3>Mistake 4: Forgetting fallback content</h3>

            <p>
                Providing fallback text gives users a useful message when the
                media element cannot be played.
            </p>

            <h2>20. Best Practices</h2>

            <ul>
                <li>Use <code>controls</code> when users need to control playback.</li>
                <li>Provide appropriate media formats and MIME types.</li>
                <li>Use <code>poster</code> images for videos when a preview is useful.</li>
                <li>Do not depend on autoplay for important content.</li>
                <li>Provide fallback text where appropriate.</li>
                <li>Use captions and accessibility features for video content when required.</li>
                <li>Keep media file sizes optimized for reasonable loading performance.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>21. Practice Example</h2>

            <p>
                Create a simple HTML page containing one audio lesson and one
                training video.
            </p>

            <pre><code>&lt;h2&gt;
    HTML Audio Lesson
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;audio controls&gt;

    &lt;source src="lesson.mp3"
            type="audio/mpeg"&gt;

    Your browser does not support audio.

&lt;/audio&gt;


&lt;h2&gt;
    HTML Video Lesson
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;video controls
       width="600"
       poster="lesson.jpg"&gt;

    &lt;source src="lesson.mp4"
            type="video/mp4"&gt;

    Your browser does not support video.

&lt;/video&gt;</code></pre>

<div class="try-it-container">
    <button type="button"
            class="try-it-btn"
            onclick="tryHTML(this)">
        Try It
    </button>
</div>

            <h3>Output</h3>

            <div class="alert alert-info mt-4">
                <h2 class="h4 fw-bold">Summary</h2>
                <p class="mb-0">
                    HTML5 provides the <code>&lt;audio&gt;</code> and
                    <code>&lt;video&gt;</code> elements for native multimedia support.
                    The <code>controls</code>, <code>autoplay</code>,
                    <code>loop</code>, <code>muted</code>, and <code>poster</code>
                    attributes control different parts of media behavior.
                    Using the correct source format, fallback text, and accessibility
                    support helps create a better multimedia experience.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Navigation -->
            <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-4">
                <a class="btn btn-outline-primary"
                   href="@Url.Action("NewInputTypes", "HTML5Features", new { area = "HTML" })">
                    ← Previous: New Input Types
                </a>

                <a class="btn btn-primary"
                   href="@Url.Action("Canvas", "HTML5Features", new { area = "HTML" })">
                    Next: Canvas →
                </a>
            </div>

        </div>
    </div>
</main>